fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "Robes Off, Lab Coats On",
    "body": "Chance opens by framing germ theory as an old protection racket in new clothing, arguing that a priest class which once sold protection from demons and hell simply put off the robes and put on the white lab coats, keeping the caduceus as its emblem. Arce adds that the older model at least assigned responsibility, since affliction followed something you did wrong, while the germ model of the late 1800s told a healthy, well behaved person that sickness arrives by accident, no fault of their own, and then offered a product as the fix."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Road Not Taken",
    "body": "Both argue that terrain thinking is not a French curiosity but the road the establishment declined to take, naming Claude Bernard, Florence Nightingale and Rudolf Virchow, the Prussian pathologist Chance calls the father of pathology. Arce describes her own work as environmental medicine, asking where food comes from, how clean the air is, what a job and a household do to a body, and she recounts industrial era slums, tap water and coal smoke, tenement crowding, wood alcohol sold as liquor, and distillery fed cows in alleyways whose milk gave raw milk its lasting bad name."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unpacking The Black Death",
    "body": "Turning to the objection she hears most, Arce contends the Black Death was a bundle of separate events rather than one contagion, noting that books from the late 1700s do not describe it the way modern teaching does, and that flu like symptoms in some people and blackened fingers, hands and toes in others were amalgamated into a single worldwide story. She traces the Latin plaga, meaning to strike or wound, arguing that its pestilence sense arrives with the Vulgate Bible and the ten plagues of Egypt, producing what Chance calls a retroactive historical edit through a shifted word."
  },
  {
    "title": "Genetics And The Bell Curve",
    "body": "Arce calls the science of genetics an outright fraud, reasoning that genes are only ever seen through the same instruments and methods used to claim a virus, which Chance recaps through Koch's postulates and the absence of any isolated, purified particle shown to cause the symptoms attributed to it. She grants inherited traits and epigenetic influence while asking why her sister is blonde, points to Mendel's peas, junk DNA, placebo and nocebo effects, and repeatable results that hold only inside a bell curve. Chance ties it to what he calls worldview warfare, alongside a history he considers shakier than the science."
  },
  {
    "title": "Old Books And Young Terrain",
    "body": "The pair trade antiquarian enthusiasms, Chance for Webster's 1828 dictionary and Arce for the 1771 Encyclopaedia Britannica, the old naturopaths Adolf Just, Louis Kuhne, Benedict Lust and Sebastian Kneipp, Virchow's roughly two thousand publications, and her copies of the United States pharmaceutical directory, which she says thinned as ammonia, arsenic and mercury based entries replaced older plant listings. Arce describes her Substack series Navigating the Terrain and printable lessons for children, her microbiologist mother showing pond water under microscopes and starting school recycling in the nineties, and Chance links a biofield tuning session to pleomorphism in action."
  }
]

